# the_flats_04 — ROOM
#
# ⚠ This is the SOURCE; `maps/the_flats_04.json` +
# `maps/the_flats_04_markers.json` beside it are BUILT from it by
# `scripts/build_maps.sh`.  `make play MAP=the_flats_04` opens the pair.
#
# plans/34 D0.  The other three maps are STRIPS — 34 x 15, 49 x 13 and
# 30 x 17 — and `docs/PUZZLES.md` § What CANNOT be measured yet says why
# that is a wall: ***a player cannot build a round base on a map fifteen
# rows tall***.  A rail-legal curve is a RUN of hexes whose direction
# changes at most once every N (`@X356`), so a radius costs SPACE in BOTH
# dimensions, and no map in this repo had any.
#
# ⚠⚠ This map decides NOTHING.  It has no lane, no neck, no gate, no
# tower site and nothing to defend — the other three teach a shape, and
# this one is the absence of one.  `@X360`: every base that is seen is
# built by a player, so what `plans/04` owes the shape layer is ROOM.
#
# ── Why it is 65 hexes across and not more ──
#
# `numbers.json` § world.atmosphere_haze_radius is 40 — the radius the
# design says you can see — and `tests/11_f8_the_tick_budget.loft`
# measures the tick over exactly that disc.  So the size is the design's
# own bound rather than a guess: a map bigger than the haze is a map you
# cannot see across.
#
# ⚠ 4225 hexes against the disc's 4921, so the COST comparison holds.
# ⚠⚠ But this is a SQUARE and the disc is not: by `lat_distance` the
# edge of it is 32 hexes from the core and the four corners are 48, past
# the haze.  That is the right way round for a sandbox — every row and
# column through the core is visible end to end, and the corners are
# over the horizon.
#
# ⚠⚠ And the tick agrees about where the ceiling is (`@M098`): a
# FIGHTING tick on this map costs **183 ms of a 667 ms budget** against
# 12 ms on `starter_01`.  ⚠ An IDLE one is free — 81 µs here against
# 97 µs there — because with no wave running there is no field to build.
# ***The size of a map costs nothing until something is walking on it.***
#
# ⚠ One hitch you will feel: pressing **P** bakes the whole ground mesh,
# which costs ~1.4 s here against ~0.2 s on `starter_01`, and it is paid
# on every press because the watch resets when play mode is off.
#
# ── Why the floor is speckled ──
#
# ⚠⚠ **You cannot feel speed over one flat colour.**  `ground_gl.loft`
# draws one flat-unlit colour per palette kind, so a uniform field gives
# a follow camera nothing to pass and a drive across it looks like a
# still frame.  The patches are a MOTION CUE and nothing else: `sand`,
# `hill` and `rock` are all height 0.0 and all `walk_ground` +
# `walk_vehicle`, so not one of them turns, blocks or slows anything.
# ⚠ They do differ in FOOTING (`@X284` — 153 / 174 / 220 HP), which is a
# free second axis to build against, not a rule this map is making.
#
# ── The rim is the one thing that says no ──
#
# `steep_rock` carries `walk_vehicle: false` (BACKLOG C10), so two rings
# of it are a closed edge: the chassis HOVERS and open sea is free to
# cross (`@D006`), and without a rim the sandbox's real boundary would be
# the one-hex ring past the paint where the mesher stops drawing
# (`mesh_chunks.loft::mesh_hex_drawn`) — an invisible edge you fall off
# into a void.  It is flat grey ground you cannot enter, the same idiom
# `the_gap_03` builds its massif from.
#
# ⚠ It is played SOLO with the seven-wave default list, like every map
# here — and NOTHING comes until you drive onto a spawn marker
# (`wave_provoke_step`, and it wants the marker's own hex).  Two of them,
# at opposite ends, because `map_fault` demands at least one and opposite
# ends is what keeps a landing a choice (`@M091`).
#
# ── Editing it ──
#
# ⚠ The paint below is MECHANICAL — one `drag` per row, and every gesture
# is a row SEGMENT.  That is on purpose: a drag between two hexes of the
# same row is exactly that row's span, which is the one case where offset
# coordinates and a straight line agree, so nothing here needs a hex
# distance and nothing here steps a coordinate (`CLAUDE.md` § Hex
# convention — a `+ 1` on a `q` outside `lat_neighbour` is the bug).
# ⚠⚠ To resize it, change every row: the floor is q -30..30 over r
# -30..30 and the rectangle is ±32, and `tests/a2_the_maps.loft`
# § The flats is ROOM asserts BOTH numbers and the 4225 that follows.
echo the_flats_04: room — 65 hexes across, and nothing in it decides anything

# ⚠ Zoom 3, not the 1 the other three open at: `view_ppm` is 24 px/m
# divided by the zoom, so a 960 x 720 window shows ~31 x 27 hexes at zoom
# 1 and this map is 65 across.  A sandbox you cannot see the edges of is
# a sandbox you get lost in.
at 0 0 3

# ── The pan is cut out of the cliff ──
#
# ⚠ The whole 65 x 65 rectangle goes down as `steep_rock` FIRST and the
# floor is painted back over it.  A rim assembled from four sides would
# be four spans to get right and one gap would let the vehicle out; this
# way the rim is whatever the floor did not cover, by construction.
